How full, incremental, and synthetic full backups work in Commvault

1. Full Backup (Commvault Full)

Commvault behavior:
A Full backup reads all protected data directly from the client and writes it to the MediaAgent and storage.

Key points in Commvault:

Creates a baseline for future incrementals.

Can be Primary Full or Auxiliary Copy Full (for secondary storage).

Typically scheduled weekly or monthly.

Pros in Commvault:

Fastest restores (single job)

Simplest recovery chain

Required periodically to limit incremental chain length

Cons in Commvault:

Long backup window

High load on client, network, and MediaAgent

Large storage consumption

Example:
Weekly Primary Full to disk, followed by daily incrementals.

2. Incremental Backup (Commvault Incremental)

Commvault behavior:
An Incremental backup captures only blocks/files changed since the last successful backup job (full or incremental).

Key points in Commvault:

Uses change tracking when available (CBT for VMs, USN Journal for Windows, etc.).

Short backup window.

Builds a backup chain that depends on the last full.

Pros in Commvault:

Minimal client impact

Efficient for daily backups

Works well with deduplication

Cons in Commvault:

Restore may require multiple jobs

Longer restore times for older recovery points

Chain integrity is critical

Example:
Daily incrementals Monday–Saturday.

3. Synthetic Full Backup (Commvault Synthetic Full)

Commvault behavior:
A Synthetic Full is created entirely on the MediaAgent, by combining:

The most recent Full backup

All subsequent Incrementals

No data is re-read from the client.

Key points in Commvault:

MediaAgent reconstructs a new full backup set.

Requires all incrementals to be present and valid.

Often used in disk-based, deduplicated environments.

Pros in Commvault:

Eliminates client read load

Much shorter backup windows

Produces a “full-equivalent” recovery point

Cons in Commvault:

MediaAgent and storage I/O intensive

Not ideal if incrementals are missing or corrupted

Slightly more complex operationally

Example:
Weekly Synthetic Full built from daily incrementals.

__Commvault “Incremental Forever” Strategy

Commvault often promotes Incremental Forever, which combines:

One initial Full

Ongoing Incrementals

Periodic Synthetic Fulls for consolidation

Benefits:

Minimal production impact

Efficient storage usage

Still provides regular full restore points

___
Full backups read all data from the client and provide the fastest restores.
Incrementals capture only changed data and minimize backup windows.
Synthetic fulls are built on the MediaAgent by merging full and incremental backups, providing full restore points without impacting production systems.




